In article <Glioc.17044$V97.9622@newsread1.news.pas.earthlink.net>, 
Wolfe <aardwolfe@earthlink.nospam.net> says...
> One thing that surprised me looking at the readme/webpage; they say 
> Fargoal came out in 1983, before Rogue. Is that right? I always 
> thought rogue was a little older than that.

I too was a bit confounded by this.

>From the web page (<http://squidfighter.sourceforge.net/fargoal/>) 
"Former owners of a C64 certainly will remember Sword of Fargoal. It was 
created by Jeff McCord and released for the C64 by Epyx in 1983. It is a 
simple rogue-like game (preceding the first official release of Rogue 
though)."

So, not really 'came out [...] before Rogue' but '[was released before] 
the first official release of Rogue'. Though I think the author of this 
text stretches the meaning of 'official release' quite far. Rogue 
distribution dates at least as far back as 1980 (also the year most 
commonly quoted as Rogue's first). <http://www.hut.fi/
~eye/roguelike/rogue.html> says  that "[t]he BSD UNIX distribution 4.2 
in 1980 included a binary version of Rogue" - this counts very well as 
an 'official release' to me. And it is also a 'commercial release' 
AFAIK, since I think BSD UNIX was considered a commercial product then 
(and now?) (also see <http://www.mo.himolde.no/~knan/roguelike/1.html>).

So it seems that we should perhaps interpret 'official release' with 
'commercial release for the IBM PC platform' or even 'release on the 
Epyx label' in this case? Then it might be correct, since the Epyx 
release for 'IBM PC' was in 1983 
(<http://www.mobygames.com/game/versions/gameId,1743/>) or 1984 
(<http://www.the-underdogs.org/game.php?name=Rogue>, and also in-game 
dating of all IBM PC versions I could check), depending on who you 
believe. But the Epyx commercial release is predated by the Rogue 
authors' release (also for the IBM PC platform) by their own company A. 
I. Design (also commercial, no matter what it says on <http://www.the-
underdogs.org/game.php?name=Rogue+%28Epyx%29>, see 
<http://home.arcor.de/cybergoth/gamesc/rogueinterview.html> for this 
quote and more: "Originally, Rogue was released by Michael & Jon's 
company, A.I.Design."). Not really sure about the date here, see Glenn 
Wichman's own web site for the detailed story - 
<http://www.wichman.org/roguehistory.html>.

Now read the paragraph quoted from the Fargoal remake web page again, 
there is something more. 
"Former owners of a C64 certainly will remember Sword of Fargoal. It was 
created by Jeff McCord and released for the C64 by Epyx in 1983. It is a 
simple rogue-like game (preceding the first official release of Rogue 
though)."
Aha, the *C64* release of SoF was in 1983, but the *next* sentence tells 
us that the *game* SoF precedes "the first official release of Rogue". 
So in fact the year 1983 is not directly related to the 'official 
release' date of Rogue. Checking sources such as 
<http://home.arcor.de/cybergoth/gamesc/swordoffargoal.html> we see that 
the C64 SoF was ported from the earlier VIC-20 version, it is also 
stated that "[SoF] was one of the VERY FIRST C-64 games, so there was no 
Rogue and others to influence it". While the "C64 & VIC-20" manual on 
the same site has a copyright note "Sword Of Fargoal (C) 1983 Epyx" the 
main page states that SoF was "written [...] for the VIC-20 in 1982" (on 
SoF's author's own page (<http://fargoal.home.comcast.net/>) only the 
C64 version is dated, as 'published' and 'released' by Epyx in 1983), 
other sources also support the 1982 VIC-20 release date 
(<http://www.uvlist.com/game-104586>).

It is probable that SoF was written before Rogue made it to any non-
UNIX/mainframe platform and it is also very probable that SoF was 
released by Epyx before Rogue was released by Epyx, but I have a hard 
time accepting this stretching of the term 'official release', when the 
1980 BSD release is taken into account.

Still a *great* remake of a *great* game though. Just nitpicking for 
historical correctness.


